{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790966451,"nonce":"HcXuXC+1eMg1HY1JRMqUTVg643g=","title":"Every object allocation hashes its type with SipHash","message":"hlp_alloc_obj asks object_flags whether the type's instances need a host drop (OBJECT_DROPS, a HashSet\u003cusize\u003e) and object_tail how many host bytes follow them (OBJECT_TAILS, a HashMap\u003cusize, usize\u003e). Both use the default hasher, so each allocation runs SipHash over the type's address. Under caribou, which registers its face classes as drop types, a Time Profiler run of the interop bench's Haxe→Python construct cell (2026-10-02, release, M1) put 19% of the main thread's samples in core::hash::sip::Hasher::write under hlp_alloc_obj. Every Haxe allocation in a hosted program pays the same.\n\nBoth tables are keyed by a descriptor's address and read on the allocation path; a multiply-and-fold hasher serves them, with no new dependency.","files":null}]}